{"id":"f84bf537-6ed0-4a3f-bf8c-9c151cadb5ec","object":"chat.completion","created":1793331394,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Implementing. First checking logger.py imports.\n\n```python\nprint(read_file(\"deepinfer/logger.py\")[\"content\"][:1200])\n```","reasoning_content":"I have all the needed mechanisms. Let me implement.\n\n1. environ.py: add a factory method `int_or_none_min` or just use custom EnvVar lambda. Need error message containing variable name. EnvVar.__get__ wraps exception with f\"{self.name}: {e}\", so ValueError message will include the name. Good.\n\nNeed: unset -> None; set -> int parse, >=32, else ValueError. Empty string -> ValueError (int(\"\") raises ValueError, message \"invalid literal for int()...\" — wrapped includes name). Good.\n\nAdd:\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\nAnd in envs log section: `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)`.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only max_message_chars, validate; format: need to compute message after %s substitution and truncate only message body without touching record.\n\nImplementation in format(): The base `logging.Formatter.format` uses record.getMessage() which uses record.msg and args — non-destructive (getMessage doesn't mutate unless args is a dict... actually getMessage sets record.message = msg). Approach: use a copy of the record? Simpler: in format, before calling super().format(record), if max budget set, create a shallow copy of record (copy.copy) with truncated message? But record.message assignment... The task says don't mutate the shared record's msg/args/exc_info/stack_info. Using copy.copy is safest: set msg to truncated string and args to (), keep exc_info/stack_info references (not mutating). But formatting of the copy for stack_info uses same attributes, fine.\n\nBut requirement 5: keep multi-line prefix logic using record.message; the copy will have its message.\n\nPlan:\n\n```python\ndef __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n    super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n    if max_message_chars is not None:\n        if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n            raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n    self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nformat:\n```python\ndef format(self, record):\n    if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n            record = _truncate_record_copy(record, _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars))\n    msg = super().format(record)\n    ...\n```\nWait — should compute len first then truncate. _truncate_message does that check itself maybe. Simpler:\n\n```python\nif self.max_message_chars is not None and not _keep_full(record):\n    message = record.getMessage()\n    if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n        record = copy.copy(record)\n        record.msg = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        record.args = ()\n        record.message = record.msg\n```\nNote: super().format sets record.message and record.exc_text on the copy — fine.\n\nTruncation: marker = \"...<omitted>...\" length 15. remaining = budget - 15. head = ceil(remaining/2) = (remaining+1)//2, tail = remaining - head. For 32: remaining 17, head 9, tail 8 ✓.\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(message, max_chars):\n    marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail > 0 else \"\")\n```\nRemaining could be small (budget 32 min → 17 fine).\n\nDefault init: use `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Note this triggers env read at import time. That's fine — but careful: envs import in logger.py might create circular import? environ.py imports only stdlib. logger.py doesn't import environ currently? Let's check logger.py imports lines 1-32 — I haven't seen. I read 33-110 and 107-174. Need to check imports. I'll read lines 1-32. Also need datetime, threading etc. Import envs locally inside _setup_logger to be safe? Better to import at module top if no cycle. stats_logger imports logger. environ has no imports of logger. So top-level import is fine. But avoid risk: do local import inside _setup_logger.\n\nAlso note _setup_logger is executed at import time; if env value invalid (e.g., empty string), that raises ValueError at import... which is expected behavior? Tests likely check that setting bad value raises ValueError when accessing config. But import of logger would crash. Hmm, the test might set env var to \"abc\" and check `envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` raises. If logger module already imported, fine. Risky but spec says default init reads config. Use try/except? No — spec: \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\". If invalid, raising seems acceptable. But safer: the integration test may set invalid value then reload... Let's keep simple, follow spec.\n\n3. stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nAlso import copy in logger.py.\n\nLet me read logger.py lines 1-32 first."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10013,"completion_tokens":1345,"total_tokens":11358,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1312},"prompt_c